#!/usr/bin/env bash
# GPUI clips to a rectangle, never to rounded corners (rust/gpui.md,
# "Rounded corners don't clip"). So an element that rounds its corners
# and clips its children (overflow_hidden, overflow_x_hidden,
# overflow_y_hidden) promises a rounding it doesn't draw: a child that
# fills a corner shows its square corner past the rounding. This check
# finds every such element in the project's Rust files, tracked or new
# (EXCLUDE left out). The lint gate runs it in a GPUI app (uses_gpui).
#
#   .practices/bin/check-gpui-clip            # every Rust file
#   .practices/bin/check-gpui-clip FILE...    # just these
#
# One element is one builder chain: the calls at the same depth after
# `div()`, `text(12.)` or a variable, over any number of lines. A call
# inside `.child(…)` builds another element. A closure with one parameter
# that goes on with that parameter (`.when(on, |d| d.rounded(px(4.)))`,
# `.map(|this| { this.rounded_t(px(6.)) })`) is the same element. Comments
# and string literals are skipped. Not caught: an element built over
# several statements through a variable (`let mut r = …; r = r.…`), and
# one rounded in a helper function and clipped by its caller.
#
# A clip kept on purpose (the content slides, and every child that fills
# a corner rounds its own), marked on the clip's line or the one above,
# with a reason of three words or more:
#   // practices-allow clip: the row slides out of it
#
# Why: a GPUI app's Settings pane drew three small pictures of a window,
# each rounded 7 px with overflow_hidden and a square sidebar; the
# sidebar's corners showed past the picture's (2026-11-18). The HTML board
# didn't show it: CSS clips to rounded corners, GPUI's ContentMask holds
# only a rectangle.
#
# Needs python3; without it, it fails, never passes. The reading is in
# gpui-clip-audit.py, beside this script.

# shellcheck source=lib.sh
. "$(dirname "$0")/lib.sh"

if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
    files=("$@")
else
    files=()
    while IFS= read -r f; do
        [ -f "$f" ] && files+=("$f")
    done < <(rust_files)
fi
[ ${#files[@]} = 0 ] && exit 0
if ! command -v python3 >/dev/null; then
    echo "check-gpui-clip: needs python3, which isn't installed; the Rust files are unchecked."
    exit 2
fi
python3 "$PRACTICES_BIN/gpui-clip-audit.py" "${files[@]}"
